WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.153
- Feeling cold is physically painful.

00:00:07.816 --> 00:00:10.893
It hurts your fingers, it hurts your feet,

00:00:10.893 --> 00:00:12.476
it hurts your face.

00:00:15.587 --> 00:00:19.254
It's also potentially
very life threatening.

00:00:26.116 --> 00:00:29.700
My focus really is anything
cold, so if there is ice

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.319
or snow, or a cold
breeze, I wanna go there.

00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:36.702
But I have focused mostly
on the polar regions,

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:39.140
both in the Arctic and Antarctic.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.299
Only 48 people have
completed the unsupported,

00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:43.073
unaided expedition in the North Pole,

00:00:43.073 --> 00:00:46.590
compared to Mt. Everest where
6,000 people have summited.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.932
It's the most difficult
expedition on the planet,

00:00:48.932 --> 00:00:52.244
to a place that few people understand.

00:00:52.244 --> 00:00:54.629
A lot of what we see on an expedition

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:56.431
to the Arctic Ocean is nothing.

00:00:56.431 --> 00:00:57.264
It's like being on the inside

00:00:57.264 --> 00:00:58.672
of a ping pong ball, basically.

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:02.501
You can't see any sort of
differentiation from the sky,

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:04.794
to the horizon, to the snow surface.

00:01:04.794 --> 00:01:08.258
It's also incredible to
look out on a clear day

00:01:08.258 --> 00:01:12.009
and see the sheets of ice
that are rafted together

00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:14.593
like blocks, as big as houses,

00:01:14.593 --> 00:01:16.865
pressured in these
amazing, incredible shapes.

00:01:16.865 --> 00:01:20.448
You can't help but just
stop and be amazed.

00:01:23.595 --> 00:01:26.236
This is my third expedition
in the North Pole.

00:01:26.236 --> 00:01:28.815
The Arctic Ocean is changing very quickly

00:01:28.815 --> 00:01:30.385
compared to a lot of other places.

00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:32.472
Scientists estimate that the Arctic Ocean

00:01:32.472 --> 00:01:36.196
will be ice-free in less than
50 years in the summertime.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:39.454
Unfortunately, we find ourselves
not doing these adventures

00:01:39.454 --> 00:01:41.396
because they're there anymore,

00:01:41.396 --> 00:01:44.121
but because they might not
be there in the future.

00:01:44.121 --> 00:01:46.673
Our North Pole trip
realistically may be the last

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.328
of its kind in history, and so
it was very important for me

00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:51.346
to do this adventure so
that people have an idea

00:01:51.346 --> 00:01:54.171
of what this place is like right now,

00:01:54.171 --> 00:01:58.338
so that we can look back and
say this is what we once had.

